From 77ee79516b3028e51aaef39259db500dda248fc9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 19:26:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][GBM] Frame packing on nvidia-drm through buffers of
 Kodi's own where the driver refuses the window's

Issue #33. On NVIDIA, HDMI 3D frame packing never went out: the driver
refused the commit of the frame-packed mode, and Kodi took top and bottom,
then side by side. The logs of the report show the commit of the mode
refused, and at once the commit before it, of the 2D mode, refused too
once it carries the new buffer of the frame-packed window: what the
driver refuses is that buffer.

nvidia-drm registers a framebuffer with NVKMS only where its memory is
video memory (nvidia-drm-fb.c, nv_drm_framebuffer_init(): no NVKMS
surface where !isVidmem on a GPU that has video memory; a dma-buf that is
not nvidia-drm's own is imported as system memory, nvkms-kapi.c,
GetSystemMemoryHandleFromDmaBufSgtHelper()), and refuses the commit of a
framebuffer without one, in a 2D mode as in a frame-packed one
(nvidia-drm-crtc.c, plane_req_config_update(); nvidia-drm-fb.c,
nv_drm_framebuffer_get_frame_packed_surfaces()). The window's buffers of
a frame-packed mode are made by the EGL of NVIDIA's driver, through
egl-gbm, as the images of an EGLStream with the one modifier
DRM_FORMAT_MOD_LINEAR that frame packing needs; where that driver places
them is not in any source that can be read. Kodi's own scanout buffers
are made by GBM for scanout (GBM_BO_USE_SCANOUT), as those of more than 8
bits are, which nvidia-drm has scanned out on the 580 driver.

So, in a frame-packed mode of nvidia-drm only (FramePackedOwn.h):

- The window's buffer is asked first, in a test commit of the mode
  (CDRMUtils::TestFramePacked()). Where the driver takes it, or it cannot
  be asked, nothing changes, and kodi.log says so.
- Only where the driver refuses it, and with OpenGL ES, Kodi makes three
  buffers of its own (COwnScanout) of the format, size and modifier of
  the window's (8 bits, DRM_FORMAT_MOD_LINEAR, as wide as the second eye
  needs), each checked: 8-bit values written and read back, a frame
  flipped by the GPU and read back, what nvidia-drm asks of a
  frame-packed buffer (FramePackedBufferUsable()), and a test commit of
  the mode with it.
- Where the driver takes every one of them, the frames go out through
  them: drawn upright into a framebuffer object of their own and flipped
  from top to bottom by the GPU into the buffer that goes to the screen
  (the plane of a frame-packed mode takes no rotation:
  plane_config_update_frame_packed()), the lines between the eyes cleared
  as before (STEREO_GAP). The output keeps 8 bits in that mode
  (SetVideoOutput()), and the buffers go with the window of the next
  mode. A refusal of a later commit with them is not held against the
  buffers of more than 8 bits: the output stays in the mode before, and
  Kodi takes the 3D layout that comes next, as before.
- Where the driver refuses Kodi's buffer too, or it cannot be made, the
  window's buffer goes to the driver as before, and kodi.log says what
  the two answers mean: "what it refuses is not where the buffer is",
  with the lines of the kernel log to read ("Hades: HDMI 3D frame packing
  refused").

kodi.log says which buffer the frame goes out in: its size, format,
pitch, modifier and where the second eye begins, and the lines of each
eye and of the gap (1080p24: the left eye in lines 0-1079, black in
1080-1124, the right eye in 1125-2204).

Every other mode, every other driver and every 2D path are as they were:
nothing is asked or made unless the selected mode is frame packed and the
driver is nvidia-drm.
